WHAT A FINAL ENGLISH TRANSLATION OF ASSEMBLY MUST CONTAIN.

Status: experimental source-language contract, not an implemented parser,
preprocessor, interpreter, compiler, encoder, or equivalence proof.
Purpose: describe the instructions that a particular machine executable
actually contains, using exhaustive ordinary English. This document defines
the intended DESTINATION of a first decompilation, not a reconstruction of
the program's earlier source language or compilation history.

READ THIS FIRST.

The immediate experiment is to receive actual disassembled machine code
and translate the machine-level behavior into English Major C.

The first question is not "What R function was this?" or "How did a
high-level language eventually become these bytes?" The first question is:

  "What does this machine instruction do, under the exact architecture,
   execution state, environment, and input machine state that apply here?"

Answer that question, then proceed to the next instruction. Record all
information needed to explain the answer. Continue until all reachable
instructions and relevant program data in the chosen scope have been
accounted for, or until a specifically identified obstacle prevents it.

An original source program may be helpful as later context, but it is not
the source of authority for the first translation. Do not invent a history
through R, C, another intermediate representation, a virtual machine,
a compiler's optimizer, or a sequence of historical transformations.
We may investigate entirely different paths upward after we understand
the machine-level examples. Do not select those paths now.

This is language exploration, not an exercise in source-file brevity,
quick compilation, compact syntax, attractive pseudocode, or immediate
machine efficiency. A long and repetitive file is acceptable when the
repetition makes the exact semantics, human explanation, provenance,
or design alternatives inspectable.

PART ONE. WHAT IS BEING PINNED.

Two targets must be distinguished.

The SOURCE MACHINE TARGET is the architecture and execution environment
in which the original bytes have a machine-defined meaning. It may be
ARMv7-A in A32 state, ARMv7-A in T32 state, x86-64 in long mode,
or another explicitly named and independently described machine.

The ENGLISH DECOMPILATION TARGET is the intended long-form English Major C
representation of those machine-defined operations and their context.

The source machine is not selected merely by choosing a friendly name for
the output language. If the disassembly is x86-64, do not silently apply
the ARMv7-A T32 operation grant. If the disassembly is ARM32, determine
whether it is A32 or T32. ARM32 by itself does not resolve that question.
A physical ARMv7-A processor may support both encoding states, but an
instruction's interpretation depends on the state actually in effect.

The ARMv7-A T32 draft currently lives in:

  arm/thumb/Given a machine - objects and execution.english
  arm/thumb/All and only these initial operations are permitted.english
  arm/thumb/Instruction set architecture - sources and unfulfilled coverage.english

It currently grants 73 families of operations, NOT every instruction
that ARMv7-A T32 can execute. A disassembled instruction outside that
grant has not vanished from the physical machine. It requires either an
independently specified extension to the language's operation grant or
a declared unsupported-translation boundary. Never represent an
untranslated instruction as a successful translation merely because the
English vocabulary is presently too small.

No corresponding complete and independently verified A32 or x86-64
machine contract is claimed to exist in this repository. Such a
contract must be supplied before a translation on those targets is
claimed complete.

PART TWO. THE THREE DIFFERENT KINDS OF TEXT.

A translation document intentionally mixes three kinds of content.

FIRST KIND: ENGLISH THAT DEFINES EXECUTION.

This text specifies operations and machine-state transitions. It is the
only kind of text whose meaning is intended to be executed by a future
English Major C implementation. The choice of an operation, the operands,
the size of values, its preconditions, its permitted machine-state effects,
its failure or exception behavior, and its successor instruction must be
determined from this text together with the exact pinned definitions to
which it explicitly refers.

Executable English may use long descriptive operation names. Such names
are not abbreviations for unspecified behavior. Each name must have a
definition that gives its actual effects.

SECOND KIND: HUMAN AND LANGUAGE-MODEL EXPLANATION.

This text is deliberately NOT executed. It may explain why the author
selected particular words, what a processor manual says, a difficult
operand encoding, which assumptions remain unsettled, what other
translations were considered, what could go wrong, or what conceptual
pattern the program appears to exhibit.

The provisional source spelling for an explanatory block is:

  (NOTE:
      Any amount of explanatory text.
      It has no authority to change execution.
  )

Another provisional spelling is:

  (WHY? BECAUSE:
      The reasons for this particular description.
      Previous failed possibilities and better alternatives may be included.
      The reason is not a separate instruction.
  )

In these examples a closing parenthesis standing alone on its line ends
the block. That rule allows ordinary parentheses inside the explanation.
A future grammar may replace or supplement the delimiters; no grammar
has been accepted, and no parser is claimed here.

The exact important requirement is semantic, not the choice of
punctuation: the preprocessor, parser, or other eventual source processor
MUST distinguish executable source from inert explanation.

If a note is removed, expanded, reworded, moved to another harmless note
location, or translated into another human language without changing
the executable English, it MUST NOT change the permitted executions of
the program. It may change diagnostic locations, source-file length,
debug display, or the effort needed to process the source. Those
nonexecution effects must not be confused with a change in program
meaning.

A note that says "the register is zero" does not set the register to zero.
A note that says "branch to another address" does not branch.
A note containing an assembly mnemonic does not execute that mnemonic.
A note containing code that looks executable is still inert source text.
A NOTE is not an undocumented mechanism for implicit assertions,
type declarations, proofs, preprocessor macros, or permitted operations.

An explanation can be arbitrarily long. There is no current target for
ratio of executable words to explanatory words. Completeness and
inspectability matter more than compression.

THIRD KIND: EVIDENCE OF THE MACHINE-CODE ORIGIN.

Retain an exact, unmodified transcription or separately identified
byte-for-byte artifact of the actual machine code and the original
disassembly used as evidence. Record where it came from and exactly
which bytes and instruction addresses each English passage describes.

Source assembly inside an evidence block is not to be executed as
English Major C. The machine code is evidence about the original
machine program. It is not the text that the new source processor
should interpret as English instructions.

When a source listing omits raw instruction bytes, do not pretend its
mnemonic establishes the exact encoding, length, condition, or operands
in cases where the mnemonic is ambiguous.

An eventual source processor MAY discard inert explanatory text from
its executable representation. A publishing or archive process SHOULD
still retain that text and the original evidence: these serve different
purposes. Stripping for execution must not mean deleting the readable,
fully annotated source artifact.

PART THREE. THE MINIMUM EVIDENCE FOR EACH ORIGINAL INSTRUCTION.

For each instruction covered by the translation, retain or identify:

  1.  The exact original instruction address, in its actual address space,
      whenever that address is known.

  2.  Its machine-code bytes and byte length, when available, without
      synthesizing absent bytes from a possibly ambiguous mnemonic.

  3.  The exact traditional disassembly as obtained, including relevant
      prefixes, suffixes, addressing expressions, and annotations.

  4.  The architecture, execution mode, instruction-set revision, and
      relevant feature availability against which the bytes are decoded.

  5.  Whether the bytes are instruction bytes, data bytes, padding, an
      unresolved mixture, or bytes whose interpretation is context-bound.

  6.  The English Major C operation or ordered operations that represent
      the machine instruction's behavior.

  7.  Which registers, condition flags, memory locations, execution-state
      fields, or other architecturally visible state are read.

  8.  Which state elements are written, preserved, made architecturally
      unknown, or changed conditionally.

  9.  The exact sizes of quantities and operations: bytes, bits, signed
      or unsigned interpretation, extension, truncation, and endian rules.

  10. How any effective memory address is calculated, including the
      actual meaning of PC-relative, SP-relative, indexed, scaled,
      and displacement forms.

  11. What must hold for normal completion and what can produce an
      exception, trap, fault, or other architectural outcome.

  12. Where execution continues if the instruction completes normally,
      and the permitted successors if it branches, calls, returns,
      traps, or otherwise transfers control.

  13. Every relevant dependency on an exact calling convention,
      executable loader, relocation, imported function, library,
      operating-system promise, or processor feature.

  14. The particular architecture rule or pinned definition from which
      a machine-specific conclusion follows.

  15. Exactly what is not yet established when any of the previous
      facts cannot be recovered.

A translator must not fabricate register values, stack contents,
runtime memory addresses, branch conditions, return destinations,
library behavior, or external function results merely because such
values would make an English narrative smoother.

An instruction can have a complete semantic definition while its
runtime inputs are unknown. These are different questions: unknown
state does not make a known opcode undecodable.

PART FOUR. THE EXECUTION INDEX.

The already agreed definition remains:

  "Execution index: A value identifying the memory address from which
   the next instruction is to be fetched. Ordinary sequential execution
   advances this value according to the length of the instruction.
   Branching replaces it with the address of another instruction."

Use that term in English Major C. The execution index is an address,
not an ordinal instruction counter. Its progression depends on the
instruction length and the architecture's control-flow rules.

In ARMv7-A T32, a sequential instruction can occupy two or four bytes.
The T32 architectural value read from the register called PC as an
operand can differ from the abstract next-instruction address.
Do not substitute the PC operand value for the execution index.

In x86-64, instruction lengths are variable, and address calculations
using the instruction pointer have their own exact rules. Do not borrow
T32 rules for them.

Do not confuse an architectural next-instruction address with whatever
has been prefetched, speculatively fetched, decoded, or scheduled inside
a particular processor implementation. Mention prefetch and branch
prediction in a NOTE when they aid understanding. Do not grant them
architectural effects that the pinned machine contract does not grant.

PART FIVE. REGISTERS AND VALUES.

State precisely whether a value is an untyped register bit pattern,
a signed integer interpretation, an unsigned interpretation, an address,
a floating-point encoding, a mask, an instruction encoding, or data
under another explicitly defined representation.

Register names from the original assembly MAY appear in the explanation
and provenance. A long English source name may give them a clearer role,
but MUST NOT silently assign them a type or invariant unsupported by
the original instruction trace and the machine's calling convention.

Differentiate a register's value before an instruction from its value
after the instruction. Distinguish read-before-write from write-before-read
dependencies when they matter. Do not silently reuse a post-instruction
value as though it were the prior value.

Preservation is a concrete assertion. If a source instruction preserves
a flag or register, state that. If the architecture leaves some result
unspecified or marks an instruction illegal or unpredictable, do not
invent a predictable value to complete the English sentence.

A machine instruction that changes condition flags cannot be rendered
by a purportedly equivalent English operation that preserves them.
The reverse mismatch is also prohibited, even if a later branch
happens not to inspect those flags in a particular example.

For floating-point and vector instructions, spell out the chosen format,
register lane widths, rounding behavior, exceptional values, relevant
status flags, and exceptional behavior as the exact architecture requires.
Do not call an unspecified floating-point operation "add two numbers"
and leave the representation implicit. An absent floating-point
operation specification is a coverage gap, not permission to approximate.

PART SIX. MEMORY, DATA, AND EXECUTION.

The machine's memory is not just a list of language objects. At the
instruction level, explicitly preserve:

  The virtual byte addresses used by the instruction, their arithmetic,
  the access width and required alignment, relevant memory attributes,
  access permissions, endianness, and the distinction between a memory
  value and a register value.

  A load that might fault is not an unconditional total function from
  memory to register. A store that might fault is not an assertion that
  a write definitely happened. The exact instruction and architecture
  determine which effects are permitted before and after exceptional
  termination.

  A branch to an address is not proof that the address contains valid,
  executable instructions. Code and data interpretation depend on the
  actual execution state and memory permissions.

  A calling convention can describe the ordinary placement of arguments
  and results without proving that a particular function call obeyed it.
  Keep the architecture's instruction semantics separate from the ABI
  obligations of the relevant call boundary.

If memory ordering, multiple threads, atomics, signals, or asynchronous
events affect the chosen observation boundary, the translation must state
the relevant additional architecture and environment rules. A contract
limited to single-threaded execution must say so explicitly rather than
pretend to prove multithreaded equivalence.

PART SEVEN. BRANCHES, CALLS, RETURNS, AND EXTERNAL CODE.

Record conditional branch tests exactly: which flags or register bits are
tested, which original instruction created them, and what the targets are.
Distinguish a condition that is false from an instruction that was not
decoded, skipped due to a condition mechanism, or did not complete.

Keep both successors of a conditional branch visible. Do not replace
several machine-level cases with "otherwise do the right thing."

A call is an operation changing control state according to its exact
machine instruction and execution convention. A return is another
control transfer; it does not require the source text to invent a
high-level stack frame if the evidence does not support one.

An imported function may be known by a symbol or name without its
implementation being present in the disassembly. Record that boundary,
the evidence for the symbol, the calling convention, and the effects
actually established. Do not infer the complete meaning of a library
function from a plausible name. A recognizable QR or linear-model name
is helpful context, not independent proof of its machine effects.

Indirect calls, computed branches, loader-resolved references, and
branch targets not present in the selected dump must remain explicit
unknowns or separately specified external contracts.

PART EIGHT. ONE INSTRUCTION AT A TIME FIRST.

For the first translation pass, keep a traceable relationship between
each actual decoded instruction and its English account. The English
account may need several sentences or several elementary operations to
describe one machine instruction. That is acceptable.

Do not combine several original instructions into one high-level action
before their individual effects have been documented. Do not reorder
them because the resulting story reads more naturally.

After the literal translation exists, we may introduce named patterns,
verified equivalences, loops, functions, invariants, and higher-level
notions. Such later abstractions are additional results, not evidence
that the original byte sequence contained those source-level concepts.

The destination is not "restore the R program" and not "recreate a
compiler pipeline in reverse." The destination is a sufficiently
explicit English machine-behavior account from which different upward
language pathways can be explored.

PART NINE. KEEP ALL RELEVANT EXPLANATIONS AND ALTERNATIVES.

Use NOTE to explain what a passage means and what evidence supports it.
Use WHY? BECAUSE to preserve the rationale for a specific translation,
design choice, or terminology choice. Do not demand that those reasons
be executable.

Preserve alternatives considered, including ones rejected as incorrect,
ones merely unverified, and ones plausible on some machine targets but
not established for the actual target. The record should identify
WHY an option was rejected or left open, not simply omit it.

For the earlier missing-value example, existing alternatives are in:

  _/decisions/Choices for missing-value branching and execution.english

That record includes distinct ways to place checks and branches,
condition execution, handle missing values, and relate physical prefetch
to architectural control flow. It is relevant background, not a
directive to force that design onto a different machine-code specimen.

A speculative design note or performance hypothesis is not a benchmark
result. A benchmark result without the exact machine and executable
identity is not a reason to change the semantics of the translation.

Any statement about WHAT a machine instruction DOES is part of the
executable or architecture semantics and must remain checkable.
Any statement about WHY we wrote it this way may remain non-executable
and as lengthy as necessary. These statements should be visibly
separated rather than relying on the reader to guess which is binding.

PART TEN. ILLUSTRATION OF THE WRITING STYLE.

The following is an ILLUSTRATION ONLY. The mnemonic has been discussed
as an example, but no real machine-code bytes, instruction address,
specific decoded T32 encoding, or running process are attached here.
Consequently this example is not a verified translation of an actual
instruction in the forthcoming linear-model disassembly.

(SOURCE ASSEMBLY EVIDENCE:
    ldr r0, [sp, #8]
)

(NOTE:
    The traditional source uses a short mnemonic and three compact
    operand fragments. Even though a person familiar with ARM assembly
    can guess its intended operation, its exact encoding and the
    surrounding process state have not been proved by these characters.
    Treat this as an illustration of how the final source might read.
)

Given an already identified, legal instruction encoding which loads one
thirty-two-bit word from the effective address obtained by adding the
integer eight to the address held in the stack-pointer register:

    Compute the effective virtual byte address according to that
    encoding and the architecture's exact address-calculation rules.

    If the architecture permits the access and the memory read completes
    normally, read the four constituent bytes and construct the
    thirty-two-bit value according to the specified endianness.

    Place the resulting thirty-two-bit value into register zero.

    Preserve or modify every other architecturally visible state item
    exactly as required by the particular decoded encoding; do not
    infer effects from the abbreviated mnemonic alone.

    Apply the exact exceptional-access behavior when the access does
    not complete normally.

    On normal sequential completion, set the execution index to the
    address of the instruction just interpreted plus the length in
    bytes of the verified encoding.

(NOTE:
    The words above intentionally distinguish successful completion
    from faulting. They do not pretend that a load from a virtual
    address always reaches physical DRAM.
    They do not guess whether the original instruction occupied
    two or four bytes merely from the displayed mnemonic.
)

(WHY? BECAUSE:
    The purpose of English Major C is to replace hidden shorthand
    with inspectable language. Expanding one line to twenty lines
    is acceptable if those lines make distinctions a reader would
    otherwise have to reconstruct from tacit machine knowledge.
)

An eventual executable presentation should resolve the remaining
"according to" references against a pinned instruction definition.
It must not claim completeness while the exact encoding is unknown.

PART ELEVEN. THE TEMPLATE FOR A REAL MACHINE-CODE SPECIMEN.

Each actual translation file should begin by declaring:

    A specimen identifier.
    The provenance of the original executable and disassembly.
    An exact artifact revision or content hash if available.
    The architecture and execution state.
    The architecture manual and revision.
    Processor-feature and execution-environment facts that are known.
    Facts that remain unknown, rather than invented placeholders.
    The selected address range and its known code/data boundaries.
    The exact referenced machine contract and operation-grant revision.
    Which external symbols and code boundaries remain unresolved.

Then, for every original instruction in scope, record:

    Its address and exact evidence, if available.
    A long English account of its effects.
    An explicit account of exceptional and next-instruction behavior.
    NOTE and WHY? BECAUSE blocks wherever explanation is useful.
    An unresolved-information record when completeness cannot be shown.

Then provide:

    A coverage ledger mapping source instructions and bytes to the
    English account and any remaining gaps.

    A ledger of external calls, unresolved control-flow targets,
    missing architecture operations, and unknown environment contracts.

    The alternative interpretations considered, together with the
    evidence for rejecting or retaining them.

    A verification status that distinguishes the authored source
    from decoder confirmation, semantic comparison, executable
    English interpretation, compilation, and on-device execution.

A simple, explicit coverage status vocabulary is acceptable:
TRANSLATED AND VERIFIED, TRANSLATED BUT NOT VERIFIED,
PARTIALLY TRANSLATED, NOT YET DECODED, and OUTSIDE CHOSEN SCOPE.
Do not mark an undecoded instruction verified merely because the
surrounding high-level purpose appears obvious.

PART TWELVE. WHAT COUNTS AS A COMPLETE FIRST PASS.

For a declared finite set of original machine instructions, completion
requires that no original instruction inside that selected scope is
silently omitted, that each known encoding and state effect has an
individually traceable English account, that the chosen architecture
rules have exact provenance, and that all unresolved operands, control
flow, external calls, and unimplemented language operations are reported.

A translation containing unresolved architectural behavior may be
a useful PARTIAL specimen. It is not a complete faithful executable
decompilation. Completeness must not be inferred from eloquence,
length, apparent algorithmic plausibility, or a successful parser run.

A future semantic equivalence check should compare observables under a
declared machine state and environment, including changed registers,
memory, flags, control flow, exceptions, and applicable external
effects. Cases that the specification leaves unknown must not be
silently scored as equivalent.

The preprocessor's handling of NOTE and WHY? BECAUSE may be tested
separately: inserting or deleting inert text must leave the executable
state transitions unchanged. Human readability may improve without
any effect on what the machine is allowed to do.

No current requirement minimizes source length, number of notes,
number of words in operation names, compilation time, or runtime
instruction count. Those are future engineering choices, not the
criterion for this exploratory first language.

PART THIRTEEN. RELATION TO FUTURE LANGUAGE WORK.

This exercise can later motivate abstractions, a parser, an elaborator,
a different intermediate representation, proofs of machine-level
equivalence, or a frontend experiment informed by Idris and Idriç.

None of those directions is assumed to be the necessary route. A
translation from low-level to high-level language is an open research
question here, not an instruction to reproduce how R or any other
source-language program was initially compiled.

Keep the fully annotated bottom-level English specimen even if a much
shorter and more elegant program is eventually found. The long file
preserves evidence and decisions that the later abstraction may hide.

END OF THE DRAFT CONTRACT.
